/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  The case's ACTIVE-CHEMISTRY SELECTION: WHICH solid phases are admitted.
  AVAILABILITY, not presence -- whether an admitted solid actually appears is
  the answer, not the input.  The Ksp/dH facts live in the component records
  (CaCO3.dat solidPhases, NH4HCO3.dat solidPhases); only an operation asks for
  saturation.

  ONE is admitted here, and it does appear: the free water sits at SI +0.788
  and 1.52e-3 mol/kg of calcite precipitates to the SI = 0 ceiling.

  CaCO3 is fed as a suspended solid and admitted as CALCITE only.  Aragonite
  is declared in the component record but NOT admitted here: it is the more
  soluble polymorph (logK 1.993 vs 1.879), so admitting both without a
  nucleation rule lets the solver pick whichever the arithmetic reaches
  first.  Which polymorph forms is a KINETIC question this case does not
  model, so it is settled by declaration and said out loud.

  NH4HCO3 is NOT admitted, and the reason is the whole point of the record:
  its equilibrium is not curated.  Admitting a solid means offering the
  solver a phase it may drive to SI = 0; offering it one whose solubility
  product is an order of magnitude read off a solubility table is offering it
  a number, not a phase.  The record stays (NH4HCO3.dat, `equilibrium {
  status curationRequired; requires ( logK25 ); }`) as the named debt.  Four
  routes to that number were searched on 2026-07-28 and the record now says
  what each one hit: the public-domain databases carry no ammonium-carbonate
  solid at all; the formation datum exists only in licence-excluded sources;
  the literature Ksp is regressed against Extended UNIQUAC and is not portable
  to Davies ions; and the measured solubility sits at I ~ 2.8 mol/kg, five
  times past where Davies means anything.  The debt is therefore a
  CAMPAIGN -- a licence-clean formation datum, or Pitzer parameters for the
  NH4-HCO3 pair -- not a lookup.  It goes back in the list the day one lands,
  and not before.

  Note what this does NOT weaken: "a solid the student never wrote can enter
  the answer" still holds, and calcite is not the demonstration -- calcite is
  FED.  The demonstration waits on the datum.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
recordType    chemistrySystem;
schemaVersion 1;

equilibria
{
    solidPhases ( calcite );
}
